        h2 §How to read this documentation @87,612,624,21
        text If you already know the name of what you are looking for, the fastest way to find it is to use the at the top of the page. @87,645,624,40
        link "search button" # @87,697,624,21
        text Otherwise, you may want to jump to one of these useful sections: @87,730,624,20
        link "std::* modules" #modules2 @87,762,624,20
        link "Primitive types" #primitives3 @87,794,624,21
        link "Standard macros" #macros3 @87,827,624,20
        link "The Rust Prelude" prelude/index.html @87,859,624,20
        text If this is your first time, the documentation for the standard library is written to be casually perused. Clicking on interesting things should generally lead you to interesting places. Still, there are important bits you don’t want to miss, so read on for a tour of the standard library and its documentation! @87,891,624,81
        text Once you are familiar with the contents of the standard library you may begin to find the verbosity of the prose distracting. At this stage in your development you may want to press the “ Summary” button near the top of the page to collapse it into a more skimmable view. @87,984,624,61
        text While you are looking at the top of the page, also notice the “Source” link. Rust’s API documentation comes with the source code and you are encouraged to read it. The standard library source is generally high quality and a peek behind the curtains is often enlightening. @87,1057,624,61
        h2 §What is in the standard library documentation? @87,1130,624,22
        text First of all, The Rust Standard Library is divided into a number of focused modules, . These modules are the bedrock upon which all of Rust is forged, and they have mighty names like and . Modules’ documentation typically includes an overview of the module along with examples, and are a smart place to start familiarizing yourself with the library. @87,1164,624,81
        link "all listed further down this page" #modules3 @87,1257,624,20
        link std::slice slice/index.html @87,1289,624,20
        link std::cmp cmp/index.html @87,1321,624,21
        text Second, implicit methods on are documented here. This can be a source of confusion for two reasons: @87,1354,624,40
        link "primitive types" ../book/ch03-02-data-types.html @87,1406,624,21
        text While primitives are implemented by the compiler, the standard library implements methods directly on the primitive types (and it is the only library that does so), which are . @87,1439,624,40
        link "documented in the section on primitives" #primitives4 @87,1491,624,20
        text The standard library exports many modules with the same name as primitive types. These define additional items related to the primitive type, but not the all-important methods. @87,1523,624,41
        text So for example there is a that lists all the methods that can be called on characters (very useful), and there is a that documents iterator and error types created by these methods (rarely useful). @87,1576,624,61
        link "page for the primitive type char" primitive.char.html @87,1649,624,20
        link "page for the module std::char" char/index.html @87,1681,624,21
        text Note the documentation for the primitives and (also called ‘slice’). Many method calls on and are actually calls to methods on and respectively, via . @87,1714,624,40
        link str primitive.str.html @87,1766,624,20
        link [T] primitive.slice.html @87,1798,624,21
        link String string/struct.String.html @87,1831,624,20
        link Vec<T> vec/struct.Vec.html @87,1863,624,20
        link str primitive.str.html @87,1895,624,21
        link [T] primitive.slice.html @87,1928,624,20
        link "deref coercions" ../book/ch15-02-deref.html#implicit-deref-coercions-with-functions-and-methods @87,1960,624,20
        text Third, the standard library defines , a small collection of items - mostly traits - that are imported into every module of every crate. The traits in the prelude are pervasive, making the prelude documentation a good entry point to learning about the library. @87,1992,624,61
        link "The Rust Prelude" prelude/index.html @87,2065,624,20
        text And finally, the standard library exports a number of standard macros, and (technically, not all of the standard macros are defined by the standard library - some are defined by the compiler - but they are documented here the same). Like the prelude, the standard macros are imported by default into all crates. @87,2097,624,82
        link "lists them on this page" #macros4 @87,2191,624,20
        h2 §Contributing changes to the documentation @87,2223,624,21
        text Check out the Rust contribution guidelines . The source for this documentation can be found on in the ‘library/std/’ directory. To contribute changes, make sure you read the guidelines first, then submit pull-requests for your suggested changes. @87,2256,624,61
        row @87,2329,624,20
          link here https://rustc-dev-guide.rust-lang.org/contributing.html#writing-documentation @87,2329,30,20
          link GitHub https://github.com/rust-lang/rust @129,2329,46,20
        text "Contributions are appreciated! If you see a part of the docs that can be improved, submit a PR, or chat with us first on  #t-libs." @87,2361,624,41
        link Zulip https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/219381-t-libs/ @87,2414,624,20
        h2 §A Tour of The Rust Standard Library @87,2446,624,22
        text The rest of this crate documentation is dedicated to pointing out notable features of The Rust Standard Library. @87,2480,624,40
        h3 §Containers and collections @87,2532,624,19
        text The and modules define optional and error-handling types, and . The module defines Rust’s iterator trait, , which works with the loop to access collections. @87,2563,624,41
